{"name":"Cassandra’s Curse","hex":"#bb7700","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"cassandra","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Synonym of leather leaf (“Chamaedaphne calyculata”)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cassandra"},{"term":"curse","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A supernatural detriment or hindrance; a bane.","A prayer or imprecation that harm may befall someone."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To place a curse upon (a person or object).","To call upon divine or supernatural power to send injury upon; to imprecate evil upon; to execrate."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/curse"}],"entries":[{"title":"Cassandra (metaphor)","blocks":[{"text":"The Cassandra metaphor relates to a person whose valid warnings or concerns are disbelieved by others."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/42/Cassandra1.jpeg/330px-Cassandra1.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cassandra_(metaphor)","wikidata":"Q2522199"},{"title":"Curse","blocks":[{"text":"A curse is any expressed wish that some form of adversity or misfortune will befall or attach to one or more persons, a place, or an object. In particular, \"curse\" may refer to such a wish or pronouncement made effective by a supernatural or spiritual power, such as a god or gods, a spirit, or a natural force, or else as a kind of spell by magic or witchcraft; in the latter sense, a curse can also be called a hex or a jinx. In many belief systems, the curse itself is considered to have some causative force in the result. To reverse or eliminate a curse is sometimes called \"removal\" or \"breaking\", as the spell has to be dispelled, and often requires elaborate rituals or prayers."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/A_woman_makes_a_cursing_ritual_ceremony.jpg/330px-A_woman_makes_a_cursing_ritual_ceremony.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Curse","wikidata":"Q109411"},{"title":"Cassandra","blocks":[{"text":"In Greek mythology, Cassandra, also spelled Kassandra or Casandra, was a Trojan priestess dedicated to the god Apollo and fated by him to utter true prophecies, but never to be believed. Cassandra lived through the Trojan War and survived the sack of the city, but was murdered by Clytemnestra and Aegisthus when Agamemnon brought her to Mycenae as a pallake."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Attic_red-figure_cup_with_Ajax_and_Cassandra_Louvre_G_458.jpg/330px-Attic_red-figure_cup_with_Ajax_and_Cassandra_Louvre_G_458.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cassandra","wikidata":"Q170779"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/1170_-_Keramikos_Museum%2C_Athens_-_Curse_inscription_-_Photo_by_Giovanni_Dall%27Orto%2C_Nov_12_2009.jpg/500px-1170_-_Keramikos_Museum%2C_Athens_-_Curse_inscription_-_Photo_by_Giovanni_Dall%27Orto%2C_Nov_12_2009.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/1170_-_Keramikos_Museum%2C_Athens_-_Curse_inscription_-_Photo_by_Giovanni_Dall%27Orto%2C_Nov_12_2009.jpg/1280px-1170_-_Keramikos_Museum%2C_Athens_-_Curse_inscription_-_Photo_by_Giovanni_Dall%27Orto%2C_Nov_12_2009.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/1170_-_Keramikos_Museum%2C_Athens_-_Curse_inscription_-_Photo_by_Giovanni_Dall%27Orto%2C_Nov_12_2009.jpg/500px-1170_-_Keramikos_Museum%2C_Athens_-_Curse_inscription_-_Photo_by_Giovanni_Dall%27Orto%2C_Nov_12_2009.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Ancient Greek curse tablet, text written onto a lead sheet, 4th century BC, Kerameikos Archaeological Museum, Athens.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A1170_-_Keramikos_Museum%2C_Athens_-_Curse_inscription_-_Photo_by_Giovanni_Dall'Orto%2C_Nov_12_2009.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Donation_stele_with_curse_inscription.jpg/500px-Donation_stele_with_curse_inscription.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Donation_stele_with_curse_inscription.jpg/1280px-Donation_stele_with_curse_inscription.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Donation_stele_with_curse_inscription.jpg/500px-Donation_stele_with_curse_inscription.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Limestone donation-stele from Mendes, 3rd Intermediate Period, Dynasty XXII. The inscription celebrates a donation of land to an Egyptian temple, and places a curse on anyone who would misuse or appropriate the land.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADonation_stele_with_curse_inscription.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Schnorr_von_Carolsfeld_Bibel_in_Bildern_1860_104.png/500px-Schnorr_von_Carolsfeld_Bibel_in_Bildern_1860_104.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Schnorr_von_Carolsfeld_Bibel_in_Bildern_1860_104.png/500px-Schnorr_von_Carolsfeld_Bibel_in_Bildern_1860_104.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Shimei curses David, 1860 woodcut by Julius Schnorr von Karolsfeld","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASchnorr_von_Carolsfeld_Bibel_in_Bildern_1860_104.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/1172_-_Keramikos_Museum%2C_Athens_-_Curse_inscriptio